<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Reflections on the Revolution in France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1790">1790</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Reflections</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Reflections on the Revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> is a political pamphlet by the British statesman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-burke">Edmund Burke</a>, published in November 1790, which contrasts the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> with the unwritten British Constitution. Burke wrote it as a letter to the French aristocrat Charles-Jean-François Depont, drafting it after reading Richard Price's speech A Discourse on the Love of Our Country. He argued that the Revolution would end disastrously because its abstract foundations ignored the complexities of human nature and society. Thirteen thousand copies were bought in the first five weeks. It drew replies from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-wollstonecraft">Mary Wollstonecraft</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-paine">Thomas Paine</a>, and it is a defining tract of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conservatism">conservatism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/reflections-on-the-revolution-in-france.jpg" alt="Reflections on the Revolution in France" width="273" height="489" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edmund Burke (1729-1797) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BurkeReflections.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-burke">Edmund Burke</a> sat in the House of Commons for the Whigs, in close alliance with Lord Rockingham. He had defended constitutional limits on the Crown, denounced the persecution of Catholics in Ireland, supported American independence and pursued the impeachment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warren-hastings">Warren Hastings</a>, so allies and opponents alike were surprised by the force of his hostility to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>. Soon after the fall of the Bastille in 1789, Depont asked him for his impressions, and Burke replied with two letters; the longer, second one became the Reflections. By the following September it had gone through eleven editions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Burke held that liberty was a construct of law rather than licence to do as one liked, and that there were no rights without corresponding duties. He treated society as a living organism too complicated to be reduced to a deductive system, and he defended private property, tradition and prejudice, which in his phrase renders a man's virtue his habit. As a Whig he rejected divinely appointed monarchy, but he argued for gradual, constitutional reform and saw the inherited rights of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magna-carta">Magna Carta</a> to the Declaration of Right, as firmer than abstract rights. He predicted that disorder would make the army mutinous and that a popular general would become master of the republic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Most of the House of Commons disagreed with Burke, and the Whigs split into New and Old Whigs, Burke leading the latter. The book found favour with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> and the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-de-maistre">Joseph de Maistre</a>, and Hippolyte Taine repeated its arguments in the 19th century. It became the founding work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conservatism">conservatism</a> once the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reign-of-terror">Reign of Terror</a> of 1793 to 1794 and the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> seemed to bear out its predictions, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-hayek">Friedrich Hayek</a> acknowledged a debt to Burke. Historians have been more critical: Alfred Cobban judged its account of the Revolution's causes misleading, built on weak sources, though he praised it as literature and as political theory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Reflections on the Revolution in France” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Edmund Burke’s <b>Reflections on the Revolution in France</b> of 1790 blamed abstract theorising for the destruction of a traditional order, and the Terror seemed to many to confirm his warning: Condorcet, who wrote his Sketch for a Historical Picture of the Progress of the Human Mind while in…</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>Edmund Burke, in his <b>Reflections on the Revolution in France</b> (1790), defended the Revolution of 1688 as “made to preserve our ancient indisputable laws and liberties”, a model of change that kept inherited institutions.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Strengths</a></li></ul></section>
